2. Project Vision, Core Philosophy, And Anti-Lock-In Guarantees
* Primary Objective: Build an open-source, multi-platform, offline-first livestock and pedigree management application—initially tailored for New Zealand Red rabbitry management (ARBA standards) and architected for seamless extension to other livestock species.
* Eliminating Industry Pain Points: Built specifically to replace commercial apps that trap breeders behind recurring subscriptions, proprietary cloud silos, forced accounts, and data lock-in.
* Absolute Data Ownership:
   * Unencrypted Local Databases: All primary herd, breeding, lineage, and genetic data resides locally on the user's hardware in standard, unencrypted formats (SQLite on native devices; IndexedDB via WebAssembly in browsers).
   * One-Click Raw Portability: Users must always have immediate, unencumbered access to raw manual backups in open, human-readable formats (.json and .csv), plus standardized .HTM pedigree exports.
   * Physical Print Engine Support: Native capability to generate and print formal multi-generation pedigrees, cage cards, and thermal labels (including Bluetooth thermal label printers directly from Android/mobile devices) so physical records remain independent of any software vendor.
* Pocket-First Cage-Side Workflow:
   * The smartphone in the breeder's pocket at the cage door is the primary operational terminal.
   * Logging a breeding, palpation, kindling, kit count, foster event, or weight check must take seconds on the spot—preventing the memory loss and lost paper scraps that happen when breeders have to walk back to a desktop PC.
3. Spiritual Workflow Models And Domain Templates
Our design synthesizes the strongest architectural and workflow patterns from three foundational pedigree and rabbitry systems while removing all proprietary barriers:
* Kintracs (Desktop Lineage Software):
   * Template for deep, multi-generation ancestral lineage tracking, recursive pedigree tree traversal, inbreeding coefficient / kinship awareness, and structured animal record depth.
* Who's Due (Mobile App by Cherokee Flats):
   * Template for fast, pocket-sized, mobile-first barn workflows, one-handed cage-side event logging, active litter tracking, and immediate daily chore visibility.
* Evans Software (The Rabbit Register for Windows):
   * Rabbit-Specific Pedigree Depth: Ear tattoo tracking, registration numbers, ARBA variety/group classifications, legs/show wins, weights, and breeder/owner lineage metadata.
   * Color Genetics & Allele Inference Engine: Automated genotype tracking across standard rabbit coat color loci (A, B, C, D, E, En, Du, V, W, etc.), filling in known alleles from phenotype, inferring hidden recessive alleles from parents and offspring, flagging impossible genetic outcomes/conflicts, and running experimental test-breeding Punnett square simulations.
   * Automated Chore Cascading (TaskMaster Pattern): Logging a single breeding event automatically calculates and schedules downstream barn tasks (e.g., Day 14 palpation, Day 28 nest box placement, Day 31 expected kindling window, Day 42–56 weaning, and rebreeding readiness) with user-configurable day offsets.
   * De Facto Portability Standard (.HTM Exports): Generates clean, standardized HTML pedigree files (.HTM) compatible with existing rabbitry ecosystem expectations so breeders can share or migrate pedigrees effortlessly without vendor lock-in.
   * User-Owned Cloud File Sync Precedent: Mirrors Evans' proven pattern of storing the database file inside a user-controlled cloud folder (like OneDrive or Google Drive) so multiple devices share one source of truth without a central subscription server.
4. Technical Stack, Database Architecture, And Sync Strategy
* Unified Cross-Platform Framework:
   * React Native via Expo (JavaScript / TypeScript): Single unified codebase compiling cleanly to Android, iOS, desktop environments, and Web (Progressive Web App / static hosting capability).
* Zero-Server Offline-First Storage:
   * Native Mobile / Desktop: Local unencrypted SQLite via expo-sqlite.
   * Web / Browser PWA: Local IndexedDB / WebAssembly SQLite storage adapter so the exact same data model runs offline in any browser.
   * Core Schema Entities (Initial Target): Animals (ID, UUID, tattoo, name, sex, breed, variety, genotype array, sire_uuid, dam_uuid, DOB, status, cage_id, notes), Breeding Events, Litters / Kindling Records, Scheduled Chores / Tasks, Weights / Show Legs, and Cages / Locations.
* Multi-Device Synchronization (Zero Proprietary Backend):
   * User-Owned Cloud Folder Sync: Reads and writes master database/backup snapshots to a user-selected folder in Google Drive, OneDrive, or Dropbox so multiple phones (e.g., co-breeders, family members, or farm partners) stay synchronized.
   * Offline Peer-to-Peer Sync: Supports local folder-level mirroring via open-source tools like SyncThing over local Wi-Fi or a mobile hotspot when zero internet is available.
   * Deterministic Conflict Resolution: Every record and transaction uses globally unique identifiers (UUIDs) and ISO-8601 modification timestamps (updated_at) so concurrent edits made on separate phones in the barn merge cleanly without overwriting data.
* Hardware Ecosystem & Extensibility Context:
   * Primary Terminal: Pocket Android/iOS smartphone with Bluetooth thermal label printing support.
   * Secondary / Future Hardware Awareness: Architecture cleanly separates core logic from hardware interfaces so optional future integrations remain possible without bloating the core app—including desktop browsers, low-power local home/barn servers (e.g., Raspberry Pi acting as a local Wi-Fi host or barn kiosk), or microcontroller telemetry (e.g., ESP32 via Wi-Fi/WebSockets or HERO/Arduino boards via Web Serial).
* Photo And Media Handling (Explicitly Deferred):
   * Status: Open architectural question; intentionally excluded from Phase 1 core development.
   * Constraint: Do not design rigid media schemas, cloud image buckets, or local bloat pipelines yet. Keep the initial core build lean, fast, and text/data-focused until Kimberly is ready to evaluate photo workflows.
* Cross-Platform & Expo Migration Guardrails:
   * Pure Domain Core: Keep all business logic (pedigree traversal, coat color genetics/allele inference, TaskMaster chore scheduling, UUID + ISO-8601 updated_at conflict merging, and JSON/CSV/HTM/PDF data preparation) in 100% pure, framework-agnostic TypeScript with zero browser DOM (`window`, `document`) dependencies so it copies directly into Expo / React Native untouched.
   * Pluggable Storage Adapter: Route all database reads and writes through a single storage interface so browser storage (IndexedDB / WASM SQLite) can be swapped for native `expo-sqlite` by changing one adapter file.
   * Portable UI Boundaries: Keep UI components decoupled from domain logic, avoid web-only DOM hacks, and document where web elements map to React Native primitives (`View`, `Text`, `Pressable`, `TextInput`) for future migration.
   * Bite-Sized Execution: Deliver one focused file, schema piece, or helper function at a time for review before moving on.
